Texture parking keeps the packed copies in a file of the process's own instead of host memory (Minato, patch 0021).

Why: a parked texture's packed copy is its only copy (0017 drops the GPU storage), and it lived in host memory,
which phys_footprint charges and jetsam measures. A visual novel that cycles its frames for a long time grows that
store until nothing else can give way: on the iPhone malloc climbed from 983 to 1813 MiB in 15 min while the
footprint was held only because GPU memory still gave way. Clean file pages are not in the footprint, and the
kernel may evict them under pressure and read them back. The pack workers now write each packed copy (LZ4, then
the LZFSE repack) into an extent of one file in the process's temporary directory, unlinked at once (nothing is
left after an exit or a kill; class D protection on iOS), and a restore reads it back with pread. Freed extents are
reused first fit and given back to the file system (F_PUNCHHOLE). When the file cannot take a copy (it could not be
made, less than 2 GiB of disk is free, a write fails) the copy stays in host memory as before. The pack and repack
buffers are pages of their own (mmap), so they leave the footprint when they go. MINATO_ANGLE_PARK_FILE=0 keeps
every copy in host memory. ANGLEMetalParkingLine appends " file=<MiB> fwrite=<MiB> ffail=<n>".
Test: graphics/tests/angle_texture_parking.mm --store (all 240 frames parked: the footprint holds 0.2 GiB above
the start instead of 0.7 GiB, the 464 MiB of packed copies are in the file; a third round reads every frame back
bit for bit) and --store-full (a 64 MiB file limit: the copies past it stay in host memory, still bit for bit).
Upstream: no (Minato's memory policy).
